WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.378
Use a hypothetical city history archive to practise
reading a deployment diagram.

00:00:05.378 --> 00:00:08.101
A visitor requests a public exhibit page.

00:00:08.101 --> 00:00:14.741
The application reads descriptive records from a
relational database, renders the page, and includes

00:00:14.741 --> 00:00:17.729
image URLs delivered through a media service.

00:00:17.729 --> 00:00:24.170
A curator uploading a replacement image follows a
different path: authenticate the curator, check

00:00:24.170 --> 00:00:28.287
editing permission, store the object, and update its
metadata.

00:00:28.300 --> 00:00:34.005
One diagram contains both paths, but their failure
and permission requirements differ.

00:00:34.005 --> 00:00:37.455
Mark every crossing with the information it carries.

00:00:37.455 --> 00:00:43.160
The object store receives bytes, while the database
receives identifiers, captions and

00:00:43.160 --> 00:00:44.089
relationships.

00:00:44.089 --> 00:00:49.197
The browser receives a presentation rather than
unrestricted database access.

00:00:49.197 --> 00:00:55.698
If image storage succeeds but the metadata update
fails, an unreferenced object can remain; if the

00:00:55.698 --> 00:00:59.679
metadata points at a missing object, the public page
breaks.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:06.154
This small example makes interfaces concrete and
exposes coordination questions before introducing

00:01:06.154 --> 00:01:07.010
more servers.

00:01:07.010 --> 00:01:13.530
Naming an endpoint after a resource helps readers,
but does not settle these consistency or access-

00:01:13.530 --> 00:01:14.847
control obligations.

00:01:14.847 --> 00:01:20.685
An archive has a two-megabyte photograph but
displays a small preview in its search results.

00:01:20.685 --> 00:01:26.903
If one thousand searches each download the original,
that is about two gigabytes of image transfer

00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:28.490
before protocol overhead.

00:01:28.490 --> 00:01:34.264
A forty-kilobyte preview reduces the same
illustrative workload to roughly forty megabytes.

00:01:34.264 --> 00:01:40.355
Storing multiple representations spends some storage
and processing to reduce repeated transfer.

00:01:40.355 --> 00:01:46.256
The relational record can identify each
representation while object storage holds the bytes.

00:01:46.256 --> 00:01:49.463
Next distinguish a cache hit from an origin fetch.

00:01:49.463 --> 00:01:55.685
A media cache can reduce repeated origin work, but
total cost still depends on the charging model

00:01:55.685 --> 00:01:57.930
for storage, requests and delivery.

00:01:57.930 --> 00:02:04.023
Build a worksheet with quantities first and insert
current prices only when preparing an actual

00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:04.600
estimate.

00:02:04.600 --> 00:02:10.687
A historical cloud bill is evidence about one
workload and configuration, not a promise for the

00:02:10.687 --> 00:02:11.199
archive.

00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:17.606
Private images also need a permission design: an
unpredictable filename alone does not establish who

00:02:17.606 --> 00:02:18.887
may fetch an object.
